<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Melqart</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Melqart was the chief god of the Phoenician city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, whose name means 'king of the city'. Worshipped as protector of the city and its colonies, he had an annual festival of awakening, and his cult spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and Gades in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> identified him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>, and coins of Kition and other cities show the god as Heracles-Melqart.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/melqart.jpg" alt="Melqart" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Testa_di_herakles-melqart,_500-480_ac_ca,_da_cipro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the later account of Philo of Byblos, said to come from Sanchuniathon of Berytus, the creator Elion was father of the gods and married Beruth, “the city”, as other gods were linked with theirs: <b>Melqart</b> with Tyre, Chemosh with Moab, Tanit and Baal Hammon with Carthage.</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Among the key inscriptions are the sarcophagi of Ahiram, Tabnit and Eshmunazar II, the Bodashtart inscriptions and the Temple of Eshmun, the Kilamuwa Stela, the Karatepe bilingual, the Çineköy inscription, the Nora Stone, the Mdina Steles, the Cippi of <b>Melqart</b> and the Pyrgi Tablets.</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Iberian religion was polytheistic and shaped by Greek, Carthaginian and Phoenician practice, and Tanit, Baal, <b>Melqart</b>, Artemis, Demeter and Asclepius were known and worshipped in the region.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Tyre honoured <b>Melqart</b>, “king of the city”, whose “awakening” was celebrated each year at a festival that, according to Josephus, Hiram I was the first to hold.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>In Canaan, as Egyptian power declined in the Iron Age, peoples and cities gathered round patron gods: Chemosh of Moab, Milcom of Ammon, Qos of Edom, Yahweh of Israel, <b>Melqart</b> of Tyre and Eshmun of Sidon.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li><li><blockquote>Gadir was governed by its own elite under institutions modelled on Tyre, and its temple of <b>Melqart</b>, later identified with Hercules, drew pilgrims from across the Mediterranean; Hannibal visited before marching on Italy in 218 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/gadir">Gadir</a></li></ul></section>
